1
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:07,840
Last time, the atom went from
a solid ball to plum pudding.

2
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:12,700
Next, Ernest Rutherford's team
tested the Plum Pudding model.

3
00:00:13,050 --> 00:00:17,940
Hans Geiger and Ernest Marsden
fired alpha particles at gold foil.

4
00:00:18,290 --> 00:00:21,960
Alpha particles are fast,
heavy, and positive.

5
00:00:22,310 --> 00:00:25,840
They shoot out of radioactive
material on their own.

6
00:00:26,190 --> 00:00:28,920
The gold foil was much thinner than paper.

7
00:00:29,270 --> 00:00:32,530
A screen around it flashed
wherever a particle hit.

8
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:36,900
They counted the flashes in a
dark room, using a microscope.

9
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:40,590
Prediction: the positive
charge is spread thin.

10
00:00:40,940 --> 00:00:43,750
So every particle should
zip straight through.

11
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:46,910
Here's what really happened.

12
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:49,840
Most particles went straight through.

13
00:00:50,490 --> 00:00:52,590
A few bent at big angles.

14
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:57,130
And about 1 in 8,000 bounced
almost straight back!

15
00:01:00,780 --> 00:01:04,700
Rutherford said it was like firing
a giant shell at tissue paper,

16
00:01:04,740 --> 00:01:07,010
and having it bounce back and hit you!

17
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:10,310
So the atom is mostly empty space.

18
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:14,880
In the middle is a tiny, positive
center called the nucleus.

19
00:01:15,230 --> 00:01:19,640
It holds almost all the mass.
Electrons are outside it.

20
00:01:19,990 --> 00:01:25,050
Make the nucleus a baseball,
and the atom is 5 miles wide.

21
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:29,310
Plum Pudding is updated.
This is the Nuclear Model.

22
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:34,630
Later, in 1932, came one more piece.

23
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:39,680
Hydrogen has 1 proton, a
positive particle in the nucleus.

24
00:01:40,030 --> 00:01:43,720
Helium has 2 protons,
but 4 times the mass.

25
00:01:44,070 --> 00:01:48,820
James Chadwick found the missing
mass: neutrons, with no charge.

26
00:01:50,233 --> 00:01:52,943
But where exactly are the electrons?

27
00:01:53,293 --> 00:01:57,683
Physics said circling electrons
should spiral into the nucleus.

28
00:01:58,033 --> 00:01:59,653
The clue came from light.

29
00:02:00,003 --> 00:02:03,783
Hydrogen gas glows when
electricity runs through it.

30
00:02:04,133 --> 00:02:08,593
A prism, a wedge of glass,
splits that light into colors.

31
00:02:08,943 --> 00:02:11,073
White light makes a full rainbow.

32
00:02:11,423 --> 00:02:14,203
But hydrogen makes only four thin lines.

33
00:02:14,553 --> 00:02:18,173
Every element has its own
lines, like a fingerprint.

34
00:02:18,523 --> 00:02:21,873
That's why metals make
different colored flames.

35
00:02:22,223 --> 00:02:25,913
In 1913, Niels Bohr explained the lines.

36
00:02:26,263 --> 00:02:30,973
Electrons can only be on certain
levels, each with a fixed energy.

37
00:02:31,323 --> 00:02:35,493
It's like stairs. You stand
on a step, never between.

38
00:02:35,843 --> 00:02:38,993
Add energy, and an
electron jumps up a level.

39
00:02:39,343 --> 00:02:43,353
When it falls back, it
gives off one exact color.

40
00:02:43,703 --> 00:02:47,863
A small drop, from
level 3 to 2, gives red.

41
00:02:48,813 --> 00:02:54,323
A bigger drop, from 5 to 2,
gives violet: more energy.

42
00:02:55,273 --> 00:03:01,213
Nuclear is updated. This is the
Planetary Model: electrons on rings.

43
00:03:03,633 --> 00:03:06,343
Bohr's model matched hydrogen perfectly.

44
00:03:06,693 --> 00:03:11,023
But it failed for atoms with
more electrons, even helium.

45
00:03:11,373 --> 00:03:16,503
In 1924, Louis de Broglie said
electrons also act like waves.

46
00:03:16,853 --> 00:03:22,433
In 1926, Erwin Schrödinger wrote an
equation treating electrons as waves.

47
00:03:22,783 --> 00:03:27,263
Its answers are orbitals: places
an electron is likely to be.

48
00:03:27,613 --> 00:03:33,683
In 1927, Werner Heisenberg showed we
can't know an electron's exact path.

49
00:03:34,033 --> 00:03:38,033
Imagine 1,000 snapshots
of where the electron is.

50
00:03:39,583 --> 00:03:42,373
Stack them up, and you get a fuzzy cloud.

51
00:03:43,123 --> 00:03:46,653
It's thickest where the
electron is most likely to be.

52
00:03:47,003 --> 00:03:50,493
Planetary is updated.
This is the Quantum Model.

53
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:56,650
Five models. Each one
changed because of evidence.

54
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:03,730
Two laws. Cathode rays. Gold
foil. Light lines. Electron waves.

55
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:08,420
Each model made a prediction.
An experiment tested it.

56
00:04:08,770 --> 00:04:12,500
When the result didn't fit,
scientists updated the model.

57
00:04:12,850 --> 00:04:15,640
Each experiment didn't throw science away.

58
00:04:15,990 --> 00:04:18,530
It made our picture of the atom better.

